            "content": "# Agent instructions — examples/\n\nThe example projects. Each one is two things at once: a real dev loop for\nworking on the addon, and CI's acceptance fixture. The repository map is\n[../AGENTS.md](../AGENTS.md); the user-facing walkthrough for each example is\nits own `README.md`. This file is the contract — what has to stay true.\n\n## What an example is for\n\n**Documentation-by-example is the acceptance bar.** An example teaches the\naddon by showing it inside a realistic, minimal project — a small app someone\nmight plausibly have, wired to storyproof exactly the way a consumer would\nwire it, with nothing in it that exists only to make a test pass. Being CI's\nacceptance fixture is a consequence of that, not the purpose.\n\nA new example, or a new story inside one, earns its place by teaching\nsomething a real user encounters: changed pixels, a disabled story,\nviewport-vs-content framing, portal capture, a story outside the configured\n`storyRoots`, stale-approval rejection, malformed baseline metadata. Each\nscenario story carries a short comment saying what it demonstrates and what\nstoryproof should do; that comment is the documentation.\n\nThe one deliberate exclusion is **fault injection**. A story that hangs or\nfails its connection on command is harness machinery, not something a user\nhits, so it lives only in `packages/storyproof/test/fixtures/project`,\nexercised by CI's `visual` job.\n\n## One design, two frameworks\n\n`react-vite-sb10.5` and `nextjs-vite-sb10.5` carry the same **Ledgerline**\ndemo (Button/Header/Page, derived from Storybook's official scaffold, styled\nas a small invoicing dashboard) and the same `visual-fixture` /\n`outside-fixture` scenario stories.\n\n**The two demo sources are one design maintained as verbatim copies, and both\nmust render the same pixels.** That is the point of having two: one design,\ntwo framework integrations, no visual difference between them. The only\nsanctioned divergence is the framework type-import in `*.stories.tsx`. A\nshared workspace package is the obvious deduplication and is not allowed — it\nwould break the consumer job's out-of-workspace copy.\n\nCI's `lint` job enforces this with `diff -r` across both the `src/` and\n`outside/` trees (`-I '@storybook/[a-z-]*-vite'` exempts the type-import,\n`--exclude=__screenshots__` exempts per-environment baselines), so editing one\nexample without mirroring the other fails the build.\n\nDemo content must stay deterministic: system font stacks, no animation in\ncaptured states, fixed data. `.storybook/preview.ts`'s `storySort` orders the\nsidebar so the demo reads before the scenario stories.\n\n## Two tiers, both load-bearing\n\n1. **A real dev loop.** Each example is a genuine pnpm workspace member\n   (`examples/*` is in `pnpm-workspace.yaml`) depending on\n   `storyproof: workspace:*`, so a contributor sees their own working tree\n   rather than a published build. `turbo watch` rebuilds the addon and\n   **restarts every Storybook** on a source edit — necessary because Storybook\n   compiles its manager bundle once at startup and cannot hot-reload an addon.\n   Running an example directly (`cd examples/… && pnpm dev`) works via the root\n   `prepare` hook, but is **frozen-addon mode**: no turbo, so no rebuild and no\n   restart. Use it to look at an example, never to develop the addon.\n\n2. **The packed-artifact proof.** A `workspace:*` link proves nothing about\n   the npm tarball. CI's `consumer` job copies an example directory _out_ of\n   the workspace (`cp -r`, before any `pnpm install` has populated\n   `node_modules` anywhere in the checkout), points it at the exact tarball the\n   `package` job built (`pnpm pkg set dependencies.storyproof=file:<tarball>`),\n   and installs it there with `pnpm install --ignore-workspace`. Being outside\n   the repository — and therefore outside the pnpm workspace — is what makes\n   that install meaningful. `--ignore-workspace` is load-bearing even there:\n   without it, a plain install in a non-member directory under a workspace\n   silently no-ops, and `pnpm add` rewrites the ROOT lockfile.\n\nA third example pinning Storybook `~10.0.0` was tried and removed: 10.0.x\nnever registers the `storyIndexGenerator` preset the addon requires, so the\naddon fails closed there by design.\n\n## Ports\n\nAssigned explicitly, never left to Storybook. A new example claims the next\nfree index in **both** blocks:\n\n| Example              | Storybook | App server  |\n| -------------------- | --------- | ----------- |\n| `react-vite-sb10.5`  | 6106      | 6206 (Vite) |\n| `nextjs-vite-sb10.5` | 6107      | 6207 (Next) |\n\n`pnpm dev` launches both blocks (`turbo watch dev dev:app`).\n\nTwo reasons they are pinned rather than auto-selected: concurrent starts race\nfor a free port, and auto-restart would move the URL out from under an open\ntab. The reason the block starts at **6106 and not Storybook's default 6006**\nis that 6006 is routinely occupied by another Storybook on a developer machine\n(llame's, for instance) — and when the requested port is taken, `storybook dev`\ndoes not quietly relocate, it asks an interactive yes/no question\n(`build-dev.ts`, guarded by `!options.ci`). Under `turbo watch` that prompt\nblocks a persistent task waiting on stdin. Staying off the default means the\nquestion never gets asked. The examples' app servers follow the same\nphilosophy and fail loudly instead of relocating.\n\n## Baselines\n\nEach example ships container-captured baselines, staged so the panel\ndemonstrates every state on a fresh clone: most stories **Passed**, the Page\nstories **Changed** (approved before a deliberate source edit), and one story\n**New** so a reader can click Accept and watch a PNG appear in their checkout.\n\nAfter intentionally changing demo components, re-approve with\n`packages/storyproof/scripts/example-states.mjs` — its header comment holds the\nprocedure — for **both** examples, keeping the staged Page difference. CI runs\nthe same script in verify mode, so drift between demo sources and committed\nbaselines fails the build.\n",
