            "content": "# Agent instructions — packages/storyproof\n\nThe addon, published to npm as `storyproof`. The whole workflow lives inside dev Storybook: run the suite (widget) or the selected story (panel), inspect its baseline/candidate/diff images, approve the exact captured candidate.\n\nRepository map: [../../AGENTS.md](../../AGENTS.md). Product-facing detail — storage layout, capture contract, configuration — is [README.md](README.md) and [docs/](docs/). This file is the engineering map.\n\n`CLAUDE.md` is a symlink to this file.\n\n## Commands\n\n```bash\npnpm --filter storyproof build       # tsdown (ESM + declarations; publint + attw gates)\npnpm --filter storyproof test        # vitest (node unit tests, incl. the pack-inventory snapshot)\npnpm --filter storyproof test:visual # playwright acceptance suite\npnpm --filter storyproof typecheck   # tsc --noEmit\npnpm --filter storyproof lint        # oxlint --deny-warnings\n```\n\nNormal visual runs start from the Storybook **Visual tests** panel or widget, not a CLI.\n\n## Structure\n\nSplit by execution environment — the boundary is load-bearing.\n\n| Path           | Runs in                 | Contents                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    |\n| -------------- | ----------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `src/node/`    | Storybook server (Node) | `runner.ts` (`VisualTestRunner`: run/cancel/approve state machine), `capture.ts` (Chromium session, content clip, render-fingerprint probe), `environment.ts`, `container.ts` (containerized capture; measured rationale in its comments), `compare.ts` (pixelmatch policy, baseline metadata schema 2), `paths.ts`, `approval.ts`, `story-index.ts`, `server.ts` (artifact route + command endpoint) |\n| `src/manager/` | Browser (manager UI)    | `PanelView.tsx` (pure view), `Panel.tsx` / `TestProviderRow.tsx` (wiring), `state.ts`                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n| `src/shared/`  | Both                    | `protocol.ts` (commands), `results.ts` (`VisualResult` / `VisualRunState` / `VisualCaptureMode`)                                                                                                                                                                                                                             |\n| `test/`        | —                       | vitest unit tests; `acceptance/addon-suite.ts` the reusable spec; `smoke/` playwright; `fixtures/project` the fixture Storybook                                                                                                                                                                                              |\n\nEntry points are `index.ts`, `manager.tsx`, `preset.ts`, `preview.ts` — see the `exports` map in `package.json`.\n\n## Gotchas\n\n- **`node/` never imports `manager/`, and vice versa** — they run in different processes. Shared shapes go through `src/shared/`.\n- **Artifact paths are a security surface.** `paths.ts` (`assertPathSegment`, `isWithin`, `normalizeImportPath`) keeps every read and write inside the source-adjacent `__screenshots__/…` tree; a story id or import path is attacker-adjacent input. Never build an artifact path that bypasses these guards.\n- **`VisualTestRunner` holds two coupled stores** — public `state.results` (what the UI renders) and private `completed` (approval candidates, keyed `runId\\0storyId\\0environmentKey`). `approve()` requires a matching `completed` entry and a candidate-hash match or it throws stale-approval; keep the two in sync.\n- **Dev-only by construction.** Approval writes files, so the panel reports unavailable in static builds. Never add a code path that writes from a non-dev context.\n- **Don't rename the `.visual` suffix** on artifact directories — it keeps Storybook's story glob from mistaking an artifact dir for a story file.\n- **`pnpm pack` always rebuilds** via `prepack`, so a stale `dist` can never be packed. `test/pack-inventory.test.ts` packs the package and asserts the tarball ships only `dist/**`, `LICENSE`, `README.md`, and `package.json` within a 150 KiB budget — there is no separate inventory script.\n\n## Acceptance suite\n\n`test:visual` runs `test/acceptance/addon-suite.ts` against `test/fixtures/project`, copied to `test/.tmp/project` by `test/fixture-server.ts`.\n\n- `VISUAL_TEST_PROJECT_DIR=<path>` points the same suite at a real installed project instead — no copy, and the child Storybook's `cwd` becomes that directory so `storyproof/preset` resolves from its real `node_modules` rather than workspace source. CI's `consumer` job sets it to a packed-tarball install of an example ([examples/AGENTS.md](../../examples/AGENTS.md)).\n- The two fault-injection scenarios (simulated hang, forced connection failure) skip automatically when the target has no `control/state.json` — only `test/fixtures/project` carries that fixture.\n",
